gsPath`, `LegacyPiSettingsPath`, and `RequiredExtensions`/`TokyoNightPkg`/`TokyoNightThemes`.\n\n**2. Theme migration**: `internal/cmd/themes/*.json` → `internal/env/themes/`, go:embed updated. `tools_theme.go` becomes thin, calling env.\n\nWait — but the theme command (`tools_theme.go`) has `knownThemes`, `customThemeNames`, `runThemeList`, `runThemeSet`. The task says \"theme 相关（`embeddedThemes` go:embed + `ensureRickTheme`/`setTheme`/`currentTheme`/`purgeTokyoNight`/`piSettingsPath`/`ensureHideThinkingBlock`）随 env 迁移\". It explicitly lists `embeddedThemes`, `ensureRickTheme`, `setTheme`, `currentTheme`, `purgeTokyoNight`, `piSettingsPath`, `ensureHideThinkingBlock` — these go to env. It does NOT list `knownThemes`/`customThemeNames`/`runThemeList`/`runThemeSet`. So those stay in cmd (they're Cobra entry + theme selection logic).\n\nWait, but `runThemeSet` calls `bootstrapAgentSettings`, `setTheme`, `currentTheme`, `piListContains`, `piCommand`, `embeddedThemes`, `ensureRickTheme`, `purgeTokyoNight`. So `tools_theme.go` will need to call env for these.\n\nLet me re-read: \"`tools_theme.go` 变薄调用 env\". So `tools_theme.go` should become thin, calling env. It keeps the Cobra command + theme selection (knownThemes/customThemeNames/runThemeList/runThemeSet) but delegates to env for:\n- `bootstrapAgentSettings` → env.BootstrapAgentSettings\n- `setTheme` → env.SetTheme\n- `currentTheme` → env.CurrentTheme\n- `piListContains` → env.PiListContains\n- `piCommand` → env.PiCommand (or a env helper to install theme package)\n- `embeddedThemes` → env exposes theme file reading (env.ReadEmbeddedTheme or env.EnsureRickTheme... actually runThemeSet writes the embedded theme directly)\n\nHmm. Let me think about what functions to export from env for theme:\n- `CurrentTheme() string`\n- `SetTheme(theme string) error`\n- `BootstrapAgentSettings() error`\n- `PiListContains(substr string) bool`\n- `PiCommand(args ...string) *exec.Cmd`\n- `EmbeddedTheme(name string) ([]byte, error)` — read embedded theme file. Or `WriteEmbeddedTheme(name string) error`?\n\nActually, `runThemeSet` for embedded themes does:\n```go\ndata, err := embeddedThemes.ReadFile(embedded)  // embedded = \"themes/rick.json\"\nthemesDir := filepath.Join(runtime.AgentDir(), \"themes\")\nos.MkdirAll(themesDir, 0755)\nos.WriteFile(filepath.Join(themesDir, name+\".json\"), data, 0644)\n```\n\nAnd `ensureRickTheme` does similar. So I should expose an env function that writes an embedded theme by name. Let me design:\n\nIn env, keep the `embeddedThemes` embed.FS, and expose:\n- `WriteEmbeddedTheme(name string) error` — writes `themes/{name}.json` to `AgentDir()/themes/{name}.json`.\n- Or expose `EmbeddedThemeContent(embeddedPath string) ([]byte, error)`.\n\nActually to keep `runThemeSet` logic thin but still functional, I'll expose a function `EnsureThemeFile(name string) error` that writes the embedded theme (if it's an embedded theme) to the themes dir. But the `knownThemes` in cmd has `embedded: \"themes/rick.json\"` mapping to name. Hmm.\n\nLet me keep it simple and idiomatic. The cmd layer keeps `knownThemes` (theme metadata: name → pkg/embedded). The env layer exposes:\n- `CurrentTheme() string`\n- `SetTheme(theme string) error`\n- `BootstrapAgentSettings() error`\n- `PiListContains(substr string) bool`\n- `InstallNpmPackage(pkg string, w io.Writer) error` — hmm, or just use `PiCommand`.\n- `WriteEmbeddedTheme(name string) error` — writes themes/{name}.json into AgentDir/themes/.\n\nActually, let me look at what's minimal. `runThemeSet`:\n```go\nif embedded != \"\" {\n    data, err := embeddedThemes.ReadFile(embedded)\n    ...\n    themesDir := filepath.Join(runtime.AgentDir(), \"themes\")\n    os.MkdirAll(themesDir, 0755)\n    os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(themesDir, name+\".json\"), data, 0644)\n}\nif pkg != \"\" {\n    if !piListContains(filepath.Base(pkg)) {\n        // install\n    }\n}\nsetTheme(name)\ncurrentTheme() verify\n```\n\nFor env to handle the embed, I need the embedded themes to live in env. So I'll move `internal/cmd/themes/*.json` → `internal/env/themes/*.json`, and in env expose:\n\n```go\n// WriteEmbeddedTheme writes the embedded rick theme file (themes/<name>.json)\n// into the managed themes dir. Returns os.ErrNotExist if no such embedded theme.\nfunc WriteEmbeddedTheme(name string) error\n```\n\nBut `runThemeSet` uses `embedded` as \"themes/rick.json\" (the embed path). I can change the cmd's `knownThemes` to store just the theme `name` (since embed path == \"themes/{name}.json\"), or keep the embedded field but derive name.\n\nActually, simplest: env exposes `WriteEmbeddedTheme(embedPath string) error` where embedPath is \"themes/rick.json\". Let me have cmd call `env.WriteEmbeddedTheme(\"themes/rick.json\")`? But the file name written should be `name+\".json\"` where name is the theme name. For embedded themes, name == basename of embedPath (e.g. \"rick.json\" → name \"rick\", \"gh-dark-dimmed.json\" → name \"gh-dark-dimmed\"). So `WriteEmbeddedTheme(embedPath)` can write `filepath.Base(embedPath)` into themes dir. That works because the knownThemes embedded names are all `<name>.json` matching.\n\nActually wait, checking knownThemes:\n- {name: \"rick\", embedded: \"themes/rick.json\"}\n- {name: \"gh-dark-dimmed\", embedded: \"themes/gh-dark-dimmed.json\"}\n- {name: \"gh-light\", embedded: \"themes/gh-light.json\"}\n\nSo `filepath.Base(embedded)` = `{name}.json`. So env `WriteEmbeddedTheme(embedPath string) error` reads `embeddedThemes.ReadFile(embedPath)` and writes to `AgentDir()/themes/{base}`. Good.\n\nBut there's also `ensureRickTheme` which writes \"rick.json\" specifically. I can refactor `ensureRickTheme` to use `WriteEmbeddedTheme(\"themes/rick.json\")` then `setTheme(\"rick\")`.\n\nLet me design env's theme-related exports:\n- `EmbeddedThemes` is internal (unexported embed.FS).\n- `WriteEmbeddedTheme(embedPath string) error` — reads + writes.\n- `EnsureRickTheme() error` — seeds rick default theme.\n- `CurrentTheme() string`\n- `SetTheme(theme string) error`\n- `BootstrapAgentSettings() error`\n- `PurgeTokyoNight() error`\n- `EnsureHideThinkingBlock(path string) error`\n- `PiSettingsPath() string`\n- `LegacyPiSettingsPath() string`\n- `ExtensionManagedByRick(pkg string) bool`\n- `ContainsString(list []string, s string) bool`\n- `TokyoNightPkg`, `TokyoNightThemes`, `RequiredExtensions`\n\nWait, KR1 says \"迁移函数全导出版本（迁移清单须覆盖：...`containsString`...）\". It lists `containsString` (lowercase) as part of the migration list. But \"全导出版本\" means \"fully exported versions\". So `containsString` → `ContainsString`. OK.\n\nHmm, but should `ContainsString` be exported? It's a trivial helper. The KR explicitly lists it in the migration list, so yes, export it. Though exporting `ContainsString` is a bit odd for an env package, the KR is explicit.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The KR1 says: \"迁移函数全导出版本（迁移清单须覆盖：...）\". This means all the listed functions should be migrated as exported versions. So yes, all become exported.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. KR4 says: \"`tools_theme_test.go` 及共享测试 helper（`setupPiSettings`/`setupLegacyPiSettings`/`readManagedSettings`）**在各自 test 文件内复制/共享（测试工具，不导出为生产 API，避免仅测试用的生产方法）**\". This means test helpers should NOT be exported as production API. So the test helpers (setupPiSettings etc.) stay in test files (copied where needed).\n\nOK so the plan is clear. Let me design the exported API surface of `internal/env`:\n\n**env.go** (seam):\n```go\ntype RuntimeEnv interface {\n    Ensure() error\n    DeployCustomizations() error\n    CheckReady() []string\n}\n\ntype piEnv struct{}\n\nfunc NewPiEnv() *piEnv { return &piEnv{} }\n\nfunc (e *piEnv) Ensure() error { return Ensure() }\nfunc (e *piEnv) DeployCustomizations() error { return DeployRickCustomizations() }\nfunc (e *piEnv) CheckReady() []string { return ... }\n```\n\nHmm, but `Ensure()` runs the full init-pi and prints to stdout. Let me think about the relationship between the top-level functions and piEnv methods.\n\nActually, let me look at what `Ensure()` should do. The `RuntimeEnv` interface has `Ensure() error` (保证 pi 就绪). The current `runInitPi()` is the full init-pi sequence (install pi, bootstrap settings, install extensions, purge tokyo, verify, deploy customizations?). \n\nLet me design:\n- `Ensure() error` — the full \"保证 pi 就绪\" flow (pi install + settings bootstrap + extensions + purge + customizations + verification). This is essentially `runInitPi` but returns error only on fatal (pi missing). It prints the same output as before.\n- `DeployRickCustomizations() error` — deploy rick-gates + rick skills.\n- `CheckReady() []string` — returns missing functional points (empty = ready). Wait, the interface contract says `CheckReady() []string`. But `IsPIReady() (bool, []string)`. Hmm.\n\nThe task says: \"env 四职责按 `RuntimeEnv` 接口组织 `{ Ensure() error; DeployCustomizations() error; CheckReady() []string }`\".\n\nAnd KR3 says: \"`IsPIReady() (bool, []string)` 汇总所有功能点就绪判定\".\n\nSo there are two things:\n- `RuntimeEnv.CheckReady() []string` — returns missing points (empty slice = ready).\n- `IsPIReady() (bool, []string)` — free function returning (ok, missing).\n\nLet me define:\n```go\nfunc IsPIReady() (bool, []string) {\n    missing := CheckReady()\n    return len(missing) == 0, missing\n}\n\nfunc CheckReady() []string {\n    var missing []string\n    missing = append(missing, CheckPIInstalled()...)\n    missing = append(missing, CheckEcosystemExtensions()...)\n    missing = append(missing, CheckRickAgents()...)\n    missing = append(missing, CheckRickHooks()...)\n    return missing\n}\n```\n\nAnd `piEnv.CheckReady() []string { return CheckReady() }`.\n\nNow the check functions:\n- `CheckPIInstalled() []string` — checks pi binary (RuntimeBin) exists or `exec.LookPath(\"pi\")` succeeds? Actually \"pi 功能点就绪\" — pi installed means RuntimeBin exists (or pi on PATH). Let me think. `ensurePI` installs to RuntimeBin. So \"pi installed\" = `FileExists(RuntimeBin())`. But before install, pi might be on PATH. For the check, I'll check RuntimeBin exists OR `exec.LookPath(\"pi\")` succeeds. Actually simplest and matching init-pi: pi is \"installed\" if `FileExists(RuntimeBin())`. But the check should report \"missing\" (non-empty) when pi is not installed.\n\nLet me think about the test script's expectation: \"`CheckPIInstalled`/`CheckEcosystemExtensions`/`CheckRickAgents`/`CheckRickHooks` 各自就绪（返回 nil/空切片）\". So in the \"ready\" state, each returns nil/empty. And in \"not ready\", returns non-empty (missing points).\n\nFor the `TestIsPIReady` unit test in env, I'll set up a fake pi on PATH + HOME isolation and verify all checks pass.\n\n- `CheckPIInstalled() []string` — returns non-empty if pi binary missing. Check: `FileExists(RuntimeBin())` OR `LookPath(\"pi\")`. If neither, return `[]string{\"pi\"}` or a descriptive message.\n\n- `CheckEcosystemExtensions() []string` — runs `VerifyExtensions()` (pi list) and returns missing extensions. If `VerifyExtensions()` returns non-empty, report them.\n\n- `CheckRickAgents() []string` — checks agents dir? Since think/research/exporter agents are task9, what does CheckRickAgents check in task3? Hmm. The task says \"CheckRickAgents\" checks agent frontmatter files. But those are task9. For task3, I should implement CheckRickAgents to check the agents dir (or the presence of the expected agent files). But since task9 will add them, and task3 says \"think/research/exporter agent 文件本 task 不写\", CheckRickAgents should return... hmm.\n\nWait, let me re-read the test method expectation: \"`IsPIReady()` 返回 ok=true、missing 为空，且 `CheckPIInstalled`/`CheckEcosystemExtensions`/`CheckRickAgents`/`CheckRickHooks` 各自就绪（返回 nil/空切片）\".\n\nThis means in the \"ready\" scenario (init-pi succeeded + DeployRickCustomizations done), ALL FOUR checks return empty. So CheckRickAgents must return empty when... the agents are deployed? But task3 doesn't write agent files.\n\nHmm. This is a design tension. Let me think.\n\nOption A: `CheckRickAgents` checks the presence of agent files in `AgentDir()/agents/`. Since task9 writes them, and task3's `DeployRickCustomizations` doesn't write agents, then `CheckRickAgents` would return non-empty (missing agents) in task3's ready state. But the test method says it should be empty (ready).\n\nWait, but actually the test method says the \"ready\" state is after `init-pi` succeeds. But `DeployRickCustomizations` is part of `Ensure()` (init-pi). If `DeployRickCustomizations` in task3 writes rick-gates (extension) + rick skills but NOT agents, then `CheckRickAgents` would fail.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Maybe `CheckRickAgents` should check something that IS deployed in task3. Or maybe `CheckRickAgents` should be lenient: since agents are task9's responsibility and task3 explicitly defers them, `CheckRickAgents` could check the agents directory readiness in a way that's satisfiable now.\n\nActually, let me re-read the goal for check functions: \"4. **就绪 check 函数**：新增 `IsPIReady`/`CheckPIInstalled`/`CheckEcosystemExtensions`/`CheckRickAgents`/`CheckRickHooks`（纯「功能点就绪」，不含 session）\".\n\nAnd KR3: \"职责 4 check 函数：`IsPIReady() (bool, []string)` 汇总所有功能点就绪判定；不含任何 session 校验\".\n\nSo the four checks map to the four responsibilities:\n1. CheckPIInstalled → pi installed.\n2. CheckEcosystemExtensions → extensions installed.\n3. CheckRickAgents + CheckRickHooks → rick customizations deployed (agents + hooks).\n\nSince task3 defers agents to task9, but the checks must all be \"ready\" after `Ensure()`, I think the design should be:\n\n- `CheckRickAgents` checks the agents directory. But since agents aren't written in task3, maybe `CheckRickAgents` should check for the presence of the agents *directory* or the *expected* agent files, and `DeployRickCustomizations` in task3 should create the agents dir (empty, as a skeleton) so the check passes? But that conflicts with \"think/research/exporter agent 文件本 task 不写\".\n\nAlternatively, `CheckRickAgents` in task3 could be implemented to check a marker that IS deployed: e.g., check that `AgentDir()/agents/` exists (the directory), and `DeployRickCustomizations` creates the directory (even if empty). Then task9 fills in the files, and `CheckRickAgents` will later be extended to check the files.\n\nHmm, but that's a bit hacky. Let me think about what's cleanest and matches the test script.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole flow. The test script (task3.py) does NOT actually verify `CheckRickAgents` returns empty via a specific scenario — it runs `go test ./internal/env/... -run TestIsPIReady -v` and checks it passes. So the `TestIsPIReady` test in env is what I write, and it should pass. I control the test.\n\nThe test method says: \"`IsPIReady()` 返回 ok=true、missing 为空，且 `CheckPIInstalled`/`CheckEcosystemExtensions`/`CheckRickAgents`/`CheckRickHooks` 各自就绪（返回 nil/空切片）\".\n\nSo my `TestIsPIReady` should set up a scenario where all four checks pass (empty). For `CheckRickAgents`, I need a scenario where it returns empty.\n\nDesign decision: \n- `CheckRickAgents()` checks `AgentDir()/agents/` for the expected agent files. But since task9 writes them, and to keep task3's `TestIsPIReady` passing, I'll make `CheckRickAgents` check for the agents that rick deploys. In task3, `DeployRickCustomizations` should create the `agents/` directory as a skeleton (empty), and `CheckRickAgents` returns empty when the directory exists (or when there are no required agents yet).\n\nHmm, actually that's over-engineering. Let me think simpler.\n\nActually, the cleanest interpretation: \n- `CheckRickAgents()` checks whether the think/research/exporter agent files are present in `AgentDir()/agents/`. In task3, since these files aren't written yet, `DeployRickCustomizations` creates the agents directory structure (skeleton) but no files. \n\nBut then `CheckRickAgents` returning empty in task3 is wrong if it checks for files.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the intent is that `CheckRickAgents` and `CheckRickHooks` both check the deployment state, and in task3, since the agents are deferred, `CheckRickAgents` should be implemented to check the directory (skeleton) that task3 creates. Then task9 updates `CheckRickAgents` to check actual files.\n\nActually, you know what, let me look at this more pragmatically. The key hard requirements from the test script are:\n1. The symbols exist: `IsPIReady`, `CheckPIInstalled`, `CheckEcosystemExtensions`, `CheckRickAgents`, `CheckRickHooks`, `DeployRickCustomizations`, `type RuntimeEnv interface` with `DeployCustomizations`/`CheckReady`, `piEnv`.\n2. `TestIsPIReady` exists and passes.\n3. `CheckEcosystemExtensions` missing branch is covered (returns non-empty when an extension is missing).\n4. init-pi works end-to-end with fake binaries.\n\nThe exact semantics of `CheckRickAgents`/`CheckRickHooks` are my design choice, but they should be \"functional readiness\" checks and be consistent.\n\nLet me design a clean, consistent approach:\n\n**DeployRickCustomizations**:\n1. Deploy rick-gates extension: copy `.rick/skills/rick-gates/` → `AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/` (idempotent).\n2. Deploy rick skills: copy `.rick/skills/*/` (the skill directories, excluding rick-gates? no, rick-gates is a skill dir too...) → `AgentDir()/skills/` (idempotent).\n3. Create `AgentDir()/agents/` directory (skeleton) for task9 (empty).\n\nWait, but should I create the agents dir? The task says \"think/research/exporter agent 文件本 task 不写、完全留给 task9\". Creating an empty `agents/` dir is not \"writing agent files\", it's creating a directory. But it might be seen as a \"placeholder that task9's idempotent copy would skip\". Actually the concern in KR2 is \"避免 task3 占位文件被 task9 幂等跳过\" — i.e., avoid task3 writing placeholder agent FILES that task9's idempotent copy would then skip (thinking they're already deployed). So I should NOT write any agent files. Creating an empty dir is fine and doesn't cause that issue.\n\nHmm, but actually do I even need the agents dir? Let me keep it minimal and NOT create the agents dir in task3. Then `CheckRickAgents` needs to be designed to return empty in the \"ready\" state without requiring agent files.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me step back and think about the semantics that make the most sense:\n\nThe four check functions map to four responsibilities. Responsibility 3 (rick customizations) has three sub-parts: hook (rick-gates), skill (rick skills), agent (think/research/exporter). The check functions are `CheckRickAgents` and `CheckRickHooks` (note: only 2 check functions for responsibility 3, not 3 — skills are presumably checked implicitly or by CheckRickAgents?).\n\nActually there's no `CheckRickSkills`. So:\n- `CheckRickHooks` → rick-gates hook extension deployed.\n- `CheckRickAgents` → agents deployed (think/research/exporter).\n\nAnd rick skills are deployed but not separately checked (or folded into one of these).\n\nGiven the task3 deferral of agents, but the requirement that all four checks return empty after `Ensure()`, I think the cleanest approach is:\n\n**`CheckRickAgents()`**: checks that the agents directory contains the expected agent frontmatter files. Since task9 writes them, and task3 must have `CheckRickAgents` return empty in the `TestIsPIReady` test... \n\nOK here's my resolution: I'll make the checks check for what `DeployRickCustomizations` actually deploys, and I'll have `DeployRickCustomizations` deploy the agents as well — but wait, that contradicts \"think/research/exporter agent 文件本 task 不写\".\n\nLet me re-read KR2 one more time very carefully:\n\n\"职责 3 `DeployRickCustomizations()`：**task3 创建仓库侧 `.rick/skills/rick-gates/helper.py` 占位文件 + 目录骨架**（实际门禁校验逻辑 tasks.json 可解析/zombie/commit_hash 由 task8 填充），`DeployRickCustomizations` 幂等复制 rick-gates + rick skills 到 `runtime.AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/`；**think/research/exporter agent 文件本 task 不写、完全留给 task9**（避免 task3 占位文件被 task9 幂等跳过）\"\n\nSo `DeployRickCustomizations` in task3:\n- copies \"rick-gates + rick skills\" to `runtime.AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/`.\n\nHmm wait, \"rick-gates + rick skills 到 runtime.AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/\". This literally says copy both rick-gates AND rick skills to `extensions/rick-gates/`?? That's weird but let me consider it literally: the rick-gates \"extension\" might bundle the skills. \n\nActually, I bet this is a typo/compression in the plan. The goal section (which is more authoritative) says:\n\"把 rick-gates hook 扩展、think/research/exporter agent frontmatter、rick skills 落盘到 `~/.rick/pi/agent/`（agents/、extensions/、skills/）\"\n\nThis clearly maps:\n- rick-gates hook 扩展 → extensions/\n- think/research/exporter agent frontmatter → agents/\n- rick skills → skills/\n\nSo `DeployRickCustomizations` should:\n1. rick-gates → `AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/`\n2. rick skills → `AgentDir()/skills/`\n3. (agents → task9)\n\nI'll go with this interpretation. The KR2 \"到 runtime.AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/\" is just imprecise about the skills destination (it should be skills/, but the text only mentions the extensions path for rick-gates and glides over skills).\n\nOK so final design:\n\n**DeployRickCustomizations** (task3):\n1. Deploy rick-gates: copy repo `.rick/skills/rick-gates/` → `AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/` (idempotent).\n2. Deploy rick skills: copy repo `.rick/skills/*_skill/` (all skill dirs except rick-gates, since rick-gates is a skill dir but deployed to extensions) → `AgentDir()/skills/` (idempotent).\n\nWait, is rick-gates a skill? The repo-side is `.rick/skills/rick-gates/helper.py`. So rick-gates IS under `.rick/skills/`. But it's deployed to `extensions/rick-gates/`, not `skills/`.\n\nHmm. So when copying \"rick skills\" to `AgentDir()/skills/`, I should copy the `.rick/skills/*_skill/` directories (the actual skills), and skip `rick-gates` (which goes to extensions).\n\nActually, let me simplify: \n- rick skills = all directories under `.rick/skills/` that contain a `skill.md` file (the `*_skill` dirs). rick-gates doesn't have skill.md (it has helper.py), so it's naturally excluded from \"skills\".\n- rick-gates = `.rick/skills/rick-gates/` → extensions/rick-gates/.\n\nThis is clean: skills are `*_skill` dirs (with skill.md), rick-gates is a special dir (helper.py, no skill.md) deployed to extensions.\n\n**Check functions**:\n- `CheckPIInstalled() []string`: pi binary present (RuntimeBin exists or `exec.LookPath(\"pi\")`). Returns `[]string{\"pi runtime not installed\"}` if missing.\n- `CheckEcosystemExtensions() []string`: `VerifyExtensions()` missing extensions.\n- `CheckRickAgents() []string`: checks `AgentDir()/agents/` for expected agent files. Since task9 writes them... \n\nUgh, I keep coming back to this. Let me just decide:\n\nFor task3, `CheckRickAgents` checks whether the agents directory has the expected agent files. But since task3 doesn't write them, I need `TestIsPIReady` to pass. \n\nResolution: I'll make `CheckRickAgents` check for the presence of the agents *deployment directory* (i.e., whether `DeployRickCustomizations` has run). Since agents are deferred to task9, `DeployRickCustomizations` will create the `agents/` directory as a skeleton (empty, with maybe a `.gitkeep`), and `CheckRickAgents` returns empty when the `agents/` directory exists. Then task9 fills it and (optionally) tightens `CheckRickAgents`.\n\nActually, even simpler and more honest: `CheckRickAgents` returns empty when the agents are \"not required yet\" — i.e., in task3, there are no required agents, so it always returns empty (nil). Then task9 adds the requirement.\n\nHmm, but that makes `CheckRickAgents` a no-op in task3, which seems odd but is actually defensible: \"no required rick agents yet → check passes\".\n\nBut wait, the test method says \"CheckRickAgents ... 各自就绪（返回 nil/空切片）\". If `CheckRickAgents` always returns nil in task3, that satisfies \"就绪\".\n\nBut then what's the point of `CheckRickAgents` in task3? It's a seam — the function exists with the correct signature and semantics (returns missing agents), but since there are no agents in task3, it returns empty. Task9 will define `requiredAgents = [think, research, exporter]` and `CheckRickAgents` checks them.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest is:\n\n```go\n// requiredRickAgents are the rick custom agents deployed under AgentDir()/agents/.\n// task3 leaves this empty (think/research/exporter are deployed in task9); the\n// check is a no-op (no required agents) until they are registered.\nvar requiredRickAgents = []string{}\n\nfunc CheckRickAgents() []string {\n    var missing []string\n    for _, name := range requiredRickAgents {\n        if !FileExists(filepath.Join(AgentDir(), \"agents\", name)) {\n            missing = append(missing, name)\n        }\n    }\n    return missing\n}\n```\n\nWith `requiredRickAgents` empty, `CheckRickAgents` returns nil always in task3. Task9 sets `requiredRickAgents = []string{\"think\", \"research\", \"exporter\"}` (or similar). This is clean and honest: the check is correct, just has no required agents yet.\n\nSimilarly, `CheckRickHooks`:\n```go\nfunc CheckRickHooks() []string {\n    // rick-gates hook extension deployed under AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/.\n    if !FileExists(filepath.Join(AgentDir(), \"extensions\", \"rick-gates\")) {\n        return []string{\"rick-gates\"}\n    }\n    return nil\n}\n```\n\nWait, but what exactly does the \"hook\" check? The rick-gates extension is a directory. Let me check for the directory existence (or a marker file like index.ts/helper.py).\n\nActually, since the repo-side is `.rick/skills/rick-gates/helper.py`, the deployed form would be `AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/helper.py`. So `CheckRickHooks` checks `FileExists(filepath.Join(AgentDir(), \"extensions\", \"rick-gates\", \"helper.py\"))`.\n\nHmm wait, but pi extensions use `index.ts` not `helper.py`. But the task says rick-gates is a \"hook 扩展\" and the repo-side placeholder is `helper.py`. So the \"extension\" for rick-gates in task3 is just the helper.py (the gate logic script). Task8 fills in the real logic. The actual pi extension (index.ts) that hooks bash spawning would be... hmm.\n\nActually, I realize I'm overcomplicating. Let me reconsider what \"rick-gates hook 扩展\" means in this project. The spec says \"门禁 → 下沉 pi hook/脚本\". So the gate (门禁) is done via a pi hook (extension) + a deterministic script. The \"rick-gates\" is the name of this gate mechanism.\n\nFor task3, I just need the skeleton:\n- `.rick/skills/rick-gates/helper.py` — the gate logic script (placeholder).\n- Deploy it to `AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/`.\n\nAnd task8 fills the real logic + presumably adds the .ts hook.\n\nSo `CheckRickHooks` checks `AgentDir()/extensions/rick-gates/` (or helper.py within it).\n\nLet me settle on: `CheckRickHooks` checks that the rick-gates extension directory (with helper.py) exists:\n```go\nfunc CheckRickHooks() []string {\n    p := filepath.Join(AgentDir(), \"extensions\", \"rick-gates\", \"helper.py\")\n    if !FileExists(p) {\n        return []string{\"rick-gates\"}\n    }\n    return nil\n}\n```\n\nNow, the `TestIsPIReady` test needs all four to return empty. For `CheckRickHooks` to pass, `DeployRickCustomizations` must have been run (which deploys helper.py). For `CheckRickAgents` with empty `requiredRickAgents`, it passes trivially. For `CheckPIInstalled`, fake pi on PATH (or RuntimeBin). For `CheckEcosystemExtensions`, fake pi list showing extensions.\n\nSo `TestIsPIReady`:\n```go\nfunc TestIsPIReady(t *testing.T) {\n    t.Setenv(\"HOME\", t.TempDir()) // isolate\n    // fake pi on PATH: --version, list\n    // fake node/npm for install? No — just pre-populate so checks pass.\n    // Actually for the check test, we need:\n    //   CheckPIInstalled → pass (pi on PATH via fake)\n    //   CheckEcosystemExtensions → pass (fake pi list shows both)\n    //   CheckRickAgents → pass (no required agents)\n    //   CheckRickHooks → pass (DeployRickCustomizations creates helper.py)\n    // Deploy customizations first:\n    if err := DeployRickCustomizations(); err != nil { t.Fatal(err) }\n    // fake pi\n    ...\n    ok, missing := IsPIReady()\n    if !ok || len(missing) != 0 { ... }\n}\n```\n\nBut wait — `DeployRickCustomizations` copies from repo `.rick/skills/`. In the test environment, cwd is the repo root, so `.rick/skills/rick-gates/helper.py` should be findable. But how does `DeployRickCustomizations` find the repo `.rick/skills/`? It needs a path. \n\nHmm. The repo `.rick/skills/` is a source path. How does the compiled binary find it? The binary runs from anywhere. Options:\n1. Use `go:embed` to embed the rick-gates files and rick skills into the binary. But the skills are user-editable (dream/learning updates them), so embedding would freeze them. That's wrong for skills (they're dynamic).\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider. The task says \"rick skills 落盘到 ~/.rick/pi/agent/skills/\". The rick skills live in `.rick/skills/` (repo-side, dynamic). To deploy them, the binary needs to find the repo's `.rick/` directory.\n\nHow does rick find its `.rick/` directory? There's `internal/workspace` with `GetRickDir()` or similar. Let me check.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_7iVnDq0I16IDViCuryFn7716","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick && cat internal/workspace/paths.go && echo \"=== workspace.go ===\" && cat internal/workspace/workspace.go"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":1993,"output":8294,"cacheRead":103936,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":8209,"totalTokens":114223,"cost":{"input":0.000866955,"output":0.007215780000000001,"cacheRead":0.00037676800000000004,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.008459503}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1786905582630,"responseId":"119c3f48-e126-4dda-99c3-44e06a6d2150","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_00_7iVnDq0I16IDViCuryFn7716","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"package workspace\n\nimport (\n\t\"bufio\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\n// Path constants for the Rick workspace structure\nconst (\n\tRickDirName     = \".rick\"\n\tRickDevDirName  = \".rick_dev\"\n\tOKRFileName     = \"OKR.md\"  // deprecated: kept for migration compatibility\n\tSpecFileName    = \"SPEC.md\" // deprecated: kept for migration compatibility\n\tWikiDirName     = \"wiki\"    // deprecated: kept for migration compatibility\n\tLoopsDirName    = \"loops\"\n\tSkillsDirName   = \"skills\"\n\tDomainDirName   = \"domain\"\n\tJobsDirName     = \"jobs\"\n\tPlanDirName     = \"plan\"\n\tDoingDirName    = \"doing\"\n\tLearningDirName = \"learning\"\n\tDreamDirName    = \"dream\"\n\tDraftDirName    = \"draft\"\n)\n\n// GetHomeDir returns the user's home directory\nfunc GetHomeDir() (string, error) {\n\treturn os.UserHomeDir()\n}\n\n// getRickDirName returns the appropriate rick directory name based on the binary name\nfunc getRickDirName() string {\n\t// Get the binary name from os.Args[0]\n\tbinaryPath := os.Args[0]\n\tbinaryName := filepath.Base(binaryPath)\n\n\t// If the binary name ends with _dev, use .rick_dev\n\tif strings.HasSuffix(binaryName, \"_dev\") {\n\t\treturn RickDevDirName\n\t}\n\treturn RickDirName\n}\n\n// GetRickDir returns the path to the .rick directory in the current working directory\nfunc GetRickDir() (string, error) {\n\tcwd, err := os.Getwd()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\trickDirName := getRickDirName()\n\treturn filepath.Join(cwd, rickDirName), nil\n}\n\n// GetJobsDir returns the path to the jobs directory\nfunc GetJobsDir() (string, error) {\n\trickDir, err := GetRickDir()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(rickDir, JobsDirName), nil\n}\n\n// GetJobDir returns the path to a specific job directory\nfunc GetJobDir(jobID string) (string, error) {\n\tjobsDir, err := GetJobsDir()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(jobsDir, jobID), nil\n}\n\n// GetJobPlanDir returns the path to the plan directory for a job\nfunc GetJobPlanDir(jobID string) (string, error) {\n\tjobDir, err := GetJobDir(jobID)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(jobDir, PlanDirName), nil\n}\n\n// GetJobDoingDir returns the path to the doing directory for a job\nfunc GetJobDoingDir(jobID string) (string, error) {\n\tjobDir, err := GetJobDir(jobID)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(jobDir, DoingDirName), nil\n}\n\n// GetJobLearningDir returns the path to the learning directory for a job\nfunc GetJobLearningDir(jobID string) (string, error) {\n\tjobDir, err := GetJobDir(jobID)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(jobDir, LearningDirName), nil\n}\n\n// GetProjectName returns the project name by reading .rick/PROJECT.md (first line),\n// falling back to the module name in go.mod, then to filepath.Base(cwd).\nfunc GetProjectName() (string, error) {\n\tcwd, err := os.Getwd()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn filepath.Base(\".\"), nil\n\t}\n\n\t// 1. Try .rick/PROJECT.md first line\n\trickDirName := getRickDirName()\n\tprojectMDPath := filepath.Join(cwd, rickDirName, \"PROJECT.md\")\n\tif f, err := os.Open(projectMDPath); err == nil {\n\t\tdefer f.Close()\n\t\tscanner := bufio.NewScanner(f)\n\t\tif scanner.Scan() {\n\t\t\tline := strings.TrimSpace(scanner.Text())\n\t\t\tline = strings.TrimPrefix(line, \"#\")\n\t\t\tline = strings.TrimSpace(line)\n\t\t\tif line != \"\" {\n\t\t\t\treturn line, nil\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// 2. Try go.mod module name\n\tgoModPath := filepath.Join(cwd, \"go.mod\")\n\tif data, err := os.ReadFile(goModPath); err == nil {\n\t\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(string(data), \"\\n\") {\n\t\t\tline = strings.TrimSpace(line)\n\t\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(line, \"module \") {\n\t\t\t\tmodulePath := strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimPrefix(line, \"module \"))\n\t\t\t\tif modulePath != \"\" {\n\t\t\t\t\treturn filepath.Base(modulePath), nil\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// 3. Fallback to directory base name\n\treturn filepath.Base(cwd), nil\n}\n\n// GetRFCDir returns the path to the rfc directory under .rick/draft.\nfunc GetRFCDir() (string, error) {\n\tdraftDir, err := GetDraftDir()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(draftDir, \"rfc\"), nil\n}\n\n// GetDraftDir returns the path to the draft directory under .rick\nfunc GetDraftDir() (string, error) {\n\trickDir, err := GetRickDir()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(rickDir, DraftDirName), nil\n}\n\n// NextLoopID scans the draft/loops directory and returns the next loop_N id.\nfunc NextLoopID(draftDir string) (string, error) {\n\tloopsDir := filepath.Join(draftDir, \"loops\")\n\tentries, err := os.ReadDir(loopsDir)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tif os.IsNotExist(err) {\n\t\t\treturn \"loop_1\", nil\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"failed to read loops directory: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\n\tmaxN := 0\n\tfor _, e := range entries {\n\t\tif !e.IsDir() {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tvar n int\n\t\tif _, err := fmt.Sscanf(e.Name(), \"loop_%d\", &n); err == nil && n > 0 && n <= 9999 && n > maxN {\n\t\t\tmaxN = n\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"loop_%d\", maxN+1), nil\n}\n\n// NextJobID scans the jobs directory and returns the next job_N id.\n// If no jobs exist yet, returns \"job_1\".\nfunc NextJobID() (string, error) {\n\tjobsDir, err := GetJobsDir()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\n\tentries, err := os.ReadDir(jobsDir)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tif os.IsNotExist(err) {\n\t\t\treturn \"job_1\", nil\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"failed to read jobs directory: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\n\tmaxN := 0\n\tfor _, e := range entries {\n\t\tif !e.IsDir() {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tvar n int\n\t\tif _, err := fmt.Sscanf(e.Name(), \"job_%d\", &n); err == nil && n > 0 && n <= 9999 && n > maxN {\n\t\t\tmaxN = n\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"job_%d\", maxN+1), nil\n}\n=== workspace.go ===\npackage workspace\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n)\n\n// Workspace represents the Rick workspace manager\ntype Workspace struct {\n\trickDir string\n}\n\n// New creates a new Workspace instance and ensures the workspace exists\nfunc New() (*Workspace, error) {\n\trickDir, err := GetRickDir()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\n\tws := &Workspace{\n\t\trickDir: rickDir,\n\t}\n\n\t// Automatically ensure workspace directories exist\n\tif err := ws.EnsureDirectories(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"failed to ensure workspace directories: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\n\treturn ws, nil\n}\n\n// InitWorkspace initializes the .rick directory structure.\n// Delegates to EnsureDirectories which handles both dirs and stub files.\nfunc (w *Workspace) InitWorkspace() error {\n\treturn w.EnsureDirectories()\n}\n\n// GetJobPath returns the path to a specific job directory\nfunc (w *Workspace) GetJobPath(jobID string) (string, error) {\n\tif jobID == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"jobID cannot be empty\")\n\t}\n\treturn filepath.Join(w.rickDir, JobsDirName, jobID), nil\n}\n\n// CreateJobStructure creates the directory structure for a job\nfunc (w *Workspace) CreateJobStructure(jobID string) error {\n\tif jobID == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"jobID cannot be empty\")\n\t}\n\n\tjobPath, err := w.GetJobPath(jobID)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\t// Create job subdirectories\n\tdirectories := []string{\n\t\tjobPath,\n\t\tfilepath.Join(jobPath, PlanDirName),\n\t\tfilepath.Join(jobPath, DoingDirName),\n\t\tfilepath.Join(jobPath, LearningDirName),\n\t}\n\n\tfor _, dir := range directories {\n\t\tif err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0755); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"failed to create directory %s: %w\", dir, err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// EnsureDirectories ensures all necessary workspace directories and stub files exist.\n// Idempotent: safe to call on every plan/doing invocation.\nfunc (w *Workspace) EnsureDirectories() error {\n\tdirs := []string{\n\t\tw.rickDir,\n\t\tfilepath.Join(w.rickDir, LoopsDirName),\n\t\tfilepath.Join(w.rickDir, SkillsDirName),\n\t\tfilepath.Join(w.rickDir, DomainDirName),\n\t\tfilepath.Join(w.rickDir, JobsDirName),\n\t\tfilepath.Join(w.rickDir, DreamDirName),\n\t}\n\tfor _, dir := range dirs {\n\t\tif err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0755); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"failed to ensure directory %s: %w\", dir, err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// GetRickDir returns the .rick directory path\nfunc (w *Workspace) GetRickDir() string {\n\treturn w.rickDir\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1786905692940},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`workspace.GetRickDir()` returns `.rick` under cwd. So `DeployRickCustomizations` can use `workspace.GetRickDir()` to find `.rick/skills/`. But wait — the env package importing `internal/workspace`... is that OK? The spec says L3 (env/runtime/builder) can reuse shared path tools. workspace is L4 (infrastructure). The spec's 例外四 says \"workspace/config 跨层叶子基础设施：路径解析与配置加载可被任意层直接使用\". So env can import workspace. Good.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The task says \"env 从 `internal/runtime` import `AgentEnv`/`AgentDir`/`RuntimeDir`/`RuntimeBin`/`SettingsPath`/`EnsureAgentDir`/`FileExists`\". It doesn't mention workspace. But for DeployRickCustomizations to find the repo-side skills, I need the repo root. I can use `workspace.GetRickDir()`.\n\nHmm, but wait — does the deployment actually need to happen from the repo? The `.rick/skills/` is repo-side. When `rick tools init-pi` runs, it runs from the repo (cwd). So `workspace.GetRickDir()` = `<cwd>/.rick`. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety in the test: the test script runs `./bin/rick tools init-pi` with `cwd=repo_root`. So `workspace.GetRickDir()` = `<repo_root>/.rick`, which has the skills. Good.\n\nBut `DeployRickCustomizations` 